      discussionContent: "Speaker 1: Thanks for joining this meeting about making our office more environmentally friendly. I think the easiest first step is to add clearly labeled recycling bins near the printers, in the kitchen, and beside the main exits. Right now, people often put everything in the same bin because the recycling points are hard to find.\nSpeaker 2: I agree that better access would help, but we should also focus on reducing the amount of waste we create. We print a lot of meeting agendas and draft documents that could be shared digitally. If we used shared files and only printed final copies when necessary, we would use much less paper.\nSpeaker 1: That makes sense. We could set double-sided printing as the default and put a short reminder beside each printer. We should also make sure staff can use the document-sharing tools, because some people still save several printed copies when they are unsure how to find the latest version.\nSpeaker 2: A quick guide or short workshop could address that. It could explain how to share files, where to find recycling bins, and which materials can be recycled. We should keep the advice practical rather than making it feel like another long training session.\nSpeaker 1: Perhaps we can start with one workshop during a regular team meeting, then send a one-page guide afterward. We could also ask each team to choose someone who can answer basic questions.\nSpeaker 2: Good idea. We should measure whether the changes work by checking paper purchases and the amount of mixed waste after a few months. If the results are positive, we can add more bins or repeat the workshop for new employees.\nSpeaker 1: So our main actions are better-labeled bins, less printing through digital documents and double-sided defaults, and a short staff workshop. We can review the results later and adjust the plan."
